---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro'
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro'
import TitleBlock from '../components/TitleBlock.astro'
import { site } from '../data/site'
import '../styles/pages/about.css'

import portrait from '../assets/photos/about/portrait.jpg'
import soccerTrack from '../assets/photos/about/soccer-track.jpg'
import volunteering from '../assets/photos/about/volunteering.jpg'
import photoSample from '../assets/photos/about/photography-sample.jpg'
import workbench from '../assets/photos/about/workbench.jpg'

const facts = [
  { label: 'Name', value: site.fullName },
  { label: 'School', value: site.schoolShort },
  { label: 'Program', value: site.program },
  { label: 'Class of', value: String(site.classOf) },
  { label: 'GPA', value: site.gpa },
  { label: 'Robotics', value: 'Six years, VEX and FRC' },
  { label: 'Co-op', value: 'Dell, Process Development' },
  { label: 'Sports', value: 'Soccer, track, lifting' },
  { label: 'Volunteers', value: 'FreeKicks Special Olympics' },
  { label: 'Based in', value: site.region },
]

const description =
  'About Lucas Bianco: a vocational engineering student and robotics captain, how he got into engineering, how he works, and what he does outside the shop.'
---
<Base title="About" description={description} type="profile">
  <PageHeader title="About" measure={`class of ${site.classOf}`} />

  <section class="about-intro">
    <div class="frame about-intro-grid">
      <Figure
        src={portrait}
        alt="Portrait of Lucas Bianco"
        eager
        sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 40vw"
      />
      <div class="about-intro-text stack-md">
        <h2>Who I am</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          I'm Lucas. I'm a senior engineering student at {site.school} in Franklin, MA,
          in the {site.program} program, class of {site.classOf}.
        </p>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            I like building things that are slightly too hard for me when I start. If I already
            know how to do every part of a project, I get bored halfway through. If there's one
            piece I have no idea how to solve yet, I'll keep coming back to it until it works.
          </p>
          <p>
            Right now that means captaining a VEX team, relaunching my school's FRC team from
            nothing, running the website for a NASA HUNCH project, a co-op at Dell, and a few
            software projects at home that got bigger than I planned.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="frame">
      <div class="column prose">
        <h2>How I got into engineering</h2>
        <p>
          It started with robotics in middle school. I joined a VEX team, and by the end of it I
          was leading that team, which went to the VEX World Championship twice. That's six years of
          robotics now, running from middle school VEX to captaining my high school VEX team
          and starting an FRC team.
        </p>
        <p>
          <span class="todo">The real origin story: what first got you building, and the moment you knew you wanted engineering</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          Picking a vocational school with an engineering program was the next step. It meant
          CAD, shop time, and certifications in things like precision measurement and mechatronics
          alongside regular classes, which is a lot closer to how I like to learn.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band-dark about-how">
    <div class="frame">
      <div class="split">
        <div class="stack-md">
          <h2>How I work</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Most of my habits come from being wrong about something I was sure of.
          </p>
          <Figure
            src={workbench}
            alt="Lucas's desk and workbench with 3D printers and tools"
            caption="Where most of it happens. Desk, printers, and the pegboard wall."
            fig={1}
            sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 45vw"
          />
        </div>
        <ul class="ruled-list about-rules">
          <li>
            <h3>Measure before you believe</h3>
            <p>
              I built a forex scanner that was supposed to call short-term moves. It looked smart.
              Then I backtested it: a 46% out-of-sample win rate when it needed 55.6% just to break
              even. Real ML models only got to about 51%. I never placed a trade, and I'm glad I
              checked before I trusted it. Now I try to test the thing before I get attached to it.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>If it takes ten minutes to test, test it</h3>
            <p>
              On our FRC team, anything we can test in under ten minutes gets tested instead of
              argued about. A quick test settles things faster than a long debate, and nobody has to win the argument.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Ideas go on the board with no names</h3>
            <p>
              Another FRC rule. Once an idea is on the board, it's the team's. That way nobody
              defends a design because it's theirs, and the quiet people actually pitch things.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Write the spec, then prove it</h3>
            <p>
              For Jarvis, my home system on six Raspberry Pis, every stage starts with a written
              spec and every feature needs proof: a screenshot or a test that shows it working. It
              slows me down a little at the start and saves me a lot of "wait, did that ever work?"
              later.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="frame stack-lg">
      <div class="column prose">
        <h2>Outside of engineering</h2>
        <p>
          I've played four years of high school soccer and three years of indoor and outdoor
          track, and I lift.
          <span class="todo">One line on what sports taught you that shows up in your engineering</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          The rest of my free time goes to photography and making videos, music, building PCs,
          Pokémon cards, and hunting for good deals. That last one is a real hobby. I will spend an
          embarrassing amount of time making sure I'm not overpaying for a part.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-photo-pair">
        <Figure
          src={soccerTrack}
          alt="Lucas playing soccer for his high school team"
          caption="Four seasons of high school soccer, three of track."
          fig={2}
          sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 50vw"
        />
        <Figure
          src={photoSample}
          alt="A photograph taken by Lucas"
          caption="One of my own photos."
          fig={3}
          sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 50vw"
        />
      </div>
      <p class="small muted column">
        <span class="todo">Fig. 3 caption: say what the photo is, where you took it, and what camera</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band-tint">
    <div class="frame">
      <div class="split">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2>Volunteering with FreeKicks</h2>
          <p>
            Since 2024 I've volunteered with FreeKicks, a Special Olympics soccer program in
            Franklin, MA. We teach and play soccer with kids of all abilities.
          </p>
          <p>
            It taught me to communicate differently. The way I'd explain a drill to my own team
            doesn't work for every kid, so I had to learn to show instead of tell, break things into
            smaller steps, and read what someone needs from me before I start talking.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also taught me patience. Progress doesn't always look the same for everyone, and
            that carries over to leading a robotics team too.
          </p>
          <p>
            <span class="todo">One specific moment from FreeKicks that stuck with you</span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <Figure
          src={volunteering}
          alt="Volunteers and players on the field at a FreeKicks soccer session, shown from behind"
          caption="A FreeKicks session in Franklin. Shot from behind on purpose: no kids' faces on this site."
          fig={4}
          sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 45vw"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="frame">
      <div class="column prose">
        <h2>What I'm looking for</h2>
        <p>
          Engineering internships and co-ops. I'm already doing a co-op on Dell's Process
          Development team, alternating weeks with school, and I want more of that: real problems,
          real hardware, and people who know more than I do.
        </p>
        <p>
          <span class="todo">Where you want to go next: the field, college plans in general terms, the kind of role after high school</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          If you have something that fits, email me at <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>
          or find me on <a href={site.linkedin}>LinkedIn</a>. Resume and references on request.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="frame stack-md">
      <h2>Fast facts</h2>
      <TitleBlock items={facts} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="frame">
      <div class="column">
        <h2>Questions people ask me</h2>
        <div class="about-faq">
          <details>
            <summary>Why a vocational school?</summary>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>
                I wanted to spend real time in a shop and on CAD, not just read about it. At
                Tri-County I get both, plus certifications that mean something outside school.
              </p>
              <p><span class="todo">Your actual reason in one or two sentences</span></p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How do you have time for all of this?</summary>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>
                Honestly, not always well. Some weeks something slips. What helps is that I built
                a system for it: Jarvis puts my day, deadlines and projects on a screen on my wall, so I'm
                not deciding what to work on every time I sit down.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>VEX or FRC?</summary>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>
                VEX is where I learned to design, code, and lead. FRC is bigger in every way:
                bigger robot, bigger team, and you have to raise the money yourself. Starting an FRC
                team with no robot and no budget has taught me more about running a team than
                anything else.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Are you more hardware or software?</summary>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>
                Hardware first. Robotics, CAD, and shop work are what I want to do for a living.
                The software projects happen because I keep needing tools that don't exist yet.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What's the project you're proudest of?</summary>
            <div class="prose">
              <p><span class="todo">Pick one and say why in two sentences</span></p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What do you want to study after high school?</summary>
            <div class="prose">
              <p><span class="todo">Field of study, said in general terms (no college list)</span></p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
